rall mixed.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Negatives: relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. Strengths: reliability (conscientious), stress resilience, technical competence (weather, computer). Weakness: communication (nasty remarks) and memory for numbers (attention). Could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Weaknesses: communication, egocentric, lack of development.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Negatives: regarded as a know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: adaptability, attention, problem solving, responsibility. Weakness: crew cooperation (know-it-all, hot temper) may affect teamwork.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. Which candidate balances strengths and minimal weaknesses? Let's weigh.\n\nCandidate B: strong reliability and stress, but communication issue (nasty remarks) is serious for crew cooperation. That may be disqualifying.\n\nCandidate C: good decision, calm, technical, but egocentric, communication difficulty, unwilling to further education. Could be problematic.\n\nCandidate D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all may affect crew cooperation and professional communication.\n\nCandidate A: strong anticipation, leadership, spatial vision, but show-off and not open to new ideas. Could affect crew cooperation and adaptability.\n\nAll have some crew cooperation issues. Which is least severe? Show-off vs nasty remarks vs egocentric vs hot temper. Nasty remarks likely most severe. Hot temper also serious. Egocentric maybe similar. Show-off maybe less severe? Not open to new ideas also a problem for adaptability.\n\nWe might consider overall balance: Candidate A has strong leadership and anticipation, which are crucial for long-distance flights. The negative traits are more about attitude but may be mitigated. Candidate D has strong technical and responsibility but hot temper could be dangerous under stress.\n\nI think Candidate A might be best overall, given strong operational reliability (leadership, anticipation) and technical competence (spatial vision). The show-off and not open to new ideas are concerns but perhaps less critical than communication hostility.\n\nThus I will recommend Candidate A.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: there is none, but we can say \"Given the current facts, I propose...\"\n\nPublic message: state recommendation, reference facts, explain.\n\nMetadata vote: Candidate A.\n\nProceed."}
